"On automorphisms of undirected Bruhat graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/360080228_On_automorphisms_of_undirected_Bruhat_graphs)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; # On automorphisms of undirected Bruhat graphs ... DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2204.09174 ... This leads us to a general investigation of automorphisms of $\\Gamma(u,v)$ in the course of which we show that special matchings, which originally appeared in the theory Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials, can be characterized as certain $\\Gamma(u,v)$-automorphisms which are conjecturally sufficient to generate the orbit of e under $Aut(\\Gamma(e,v))$.\nPreprint\n\n# On automorphisms of undirected Bruhat graphs\n\n  * April 2022\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2204.09174\n\nAuthors:\n\nChristian Gaetz\n\nChristian Gaetz\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nYibo Gao\n\nYibo Gao\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest file PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe (directed) Bruhat graph $\\hat{\\Gamma}(u,v)$ has the elements of the Bruhat interval [u,v] as vertices, with directed edges given by multiplication by a reflection. Famously, $\\hat{\\Gamma}(e,v)$ is regular if and only if the Schubert variety $X_v$ is smooth, and this condition on v is characterized by pattern avoidance. In this work, we classify when the undirected Bruhat graph $\\Gamma(e,v)$ is vertex-transitive; surprisingly this class of permutations is also characterized by pattern avoidance and sits nicely between the classes of smooth permutations and self-dual permutations. This leads us to a general investigation of automorphisms of $\\Gamma(u,v)$ in the course of which we show that special matchings, which originally appeared in the theory Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials, can be characterized as certain $\\Gamma(u,v)$-automorphisms which are conjecturally sufficient to generate the orbit of e under $Aut(\\Gamma(e,v))$.\n\n\n## No file available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nRequest file PDF\n\n## Citations (0)\n\n## References (24)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCounting Spinal Tree-Child Networks via Word Encodings and Generating Functions | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/404753128_Counting_Spinal_Tree-Child_Networks_via_Word_Encodings_and_Generating_Functions)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2605.10926 ... Joan Carles Pons ... To read the file of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors.\nPreprint\n\n# Counting Spinal Tree-Child Networks via Word Encodings and Generating Functions\n\n  * May 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2605.10926\n\nAuthors:\n\nPau Vives\n\nPau Vives\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nAnna de Mier\n\nAnna de Mier\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nGabriel Cardona\n\nGabriel Cardona\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nJoan Carles Pons\n\n  * University of the Balearic Islands\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest file PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study the enumeration of spinal tree-child phylogenetic networks, a rigid family of tree-child networks in which all internal vertices lie on a single root--to--leaf path. We provide two complementary combinatorial frameworks. First, we introduce a word model: unlabeled spinal networks correspond to a suitable class of restricted words with fixed multiplicities, taken modulo a simple relabeling equivalence, which yields an explicit closed enumeration. Second, we develop a symbolic-method approach based on a marked version of trees that admits a clean recursive specification; its boxed-product translation leads to a solvable bivariate generating function and a direct derivation of the coefficients.\n\n## No file available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nRequest file PDF\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2605.10926] Counting Spinal Tree-Child Networks via Word Encodings and Generating Functions (https://accelresearching.com/accel-zinc-picolinate-50mg/?_=%2Fabs%2F2605.10926%23nLw79nwZb1b3uPkZUsgpm5M%3D)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: last month; Authors:Pau Vives, Anna de Mier, Gabriel Cardona, Joan Carles Pons ... Cite as:  | arXiv:2605.10926 [math.CO]\n\n# Mathematics > Combinatorics\n\n[Submitted on 11 May 2026]\n\n# Title:Counting Spinal Tree-Child Networks via Word Encodings and Generating Functions\n\nAuthors:Pau Vives, Anna de Mier, Gabriel Cardona, Joan Carles Pons\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Counting Spinal Tree-Child Networks via Word Encodings and Generating Functions, by Pau Vives and 3 other authors\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:We study the enumeration of spinal tree-child phylogenetic networks, a rigid family of tree-child networks in which all internal vertices lie on a single root--to--leaf path. We provide two complementary combinatorial frameworks. First, we introduce a word model: unlabeled spinal networks correspond to a suitable class of restricted words with fixed multiplicities, taken modulo a simple relabeling equivalence, which yields an explicit closed enumeration. Second, we develop a symbolic-method approach based on a marked version of trees that admits a clean recursive specification; its boxed-product translation leads to a solvable bivariate generating function and a direct derivation of the coefficients.\n\nComments:  | 27 pages, 6 figures\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2605.10926 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2605.10926v1 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.10926 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\nFrom: Joan Carles Pons Mayol [view email]\n[v1] Mon, 11 May 2026 17:56:10 UTC (33 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Gaetz_C in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AGaetz_C+in%3Amath)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; We use this to give a new proof of Marietti's conjecture in the case of lower Bruhat intervals and to prove several new cases of the parabolic conjectures. ... @misc{2204.09174, author = {Christian Gaetz and Yibo Gao}, title = {{O}n automorphisms of undirected {B}ruhat graphs}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2204.09174}, note = {arXiv:2204.09174v2} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... This leads us to a general investigation of automorphisms of $\\Gamma(u,v)$ in the course of which we show that special matchings, which originally appeared in the theory Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials, can be characterized as certain $\\Gamma(u,v)$-automorphisms which are conjecturally sufficient to generate the orbit of $e$ under $Aut(\\Gamma(e,v))$.\n\n  * The BBDVW Conjecture for Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials of lower intervals\n\nGrant T. Barkley, Christian Gaetz\n\nDec 16 2024 math.CO arXiv:2412.10256v3\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2412.10256, author = {Grant T.~Barkley and Christian Gaetz}, title = {{T}he {BBDVW} {C}onjecture for {K}azhdan-{L}usztig polynomials of lower intervals}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2412.10256}, howpublished = {International Mathematics Research Notices, Volume 2026, Issue 4, February 2026}, doi = {10.1093/imrn/rnag022}, note = {arXiv:2412.10256v3} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nBlundell, Buesing, Davies, Veličković, and Williamson (BBDVW) introduced the notion of a hypercube decomposition of an interval in Bruhat order. They conjectured a recursive formula in terms of this structure which, if shown for all intervals, would imply the Combinatorial Invariance Conjecture of Lusztig and Dyer, for Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials of the symmetric group. In this article, we prove implications between the BBDVW Conjecture and several other recurrences for hypercube decompositions, under varying hypotheses, which have appeared in the recent literature. As an application, we prove the BBDVW Conjecture for lower intervals $[e,v]$, the first non-trivial class of intervals for which it has been established.\n\n  * Poset topology, moves, and Bruhat interval polytope lattices\n\nChristian Gaetz, Patricia Hersh\n\nOct 11 2024 math.CO arXiv:2410.08076v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2410.08076, author = {Christian Gaetz and Patricia Hersh}, title = {{P}oset topology, moves, and {B}ruhat interval polytope lattices}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2410.08076}, note = {arXiv:2410.08076v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe study the poset topology of lattices arising from orientations of 1-skeleta of directionally simple polytopes, with Bruhat interval polytopes $Q_{e,w}$ as our main example. We show that the order complex $\\Delta ((u,v)_w)$ of an interval therein is homotopy equivalent to a sphere if $Q_{u,v}$ is a face of $Q_{e,w}$ and is otherwise contractible. This significantly generalizes the known case of the permutahedron. We also show that saturated chains from $u$ to $v$ in such lattices are connected, and in fact highly connected, under moves corresponding to flipping across a 2-face. When $w$ is a Grassmannian permutation, this implies a strengthening of the restriction of Postnikov's move-equivalence theorem to the class of BCFW bridge decomposable plabic graphs.\n\n  * On automorphisms of undirected Bruhat graphs\n\nChristian Gaetz, Yibo Gao\n\nApr 21 2022 math.CO arXiv:2204.09174v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2204.09174, author = {Christian Gaetz and Yibo Gao}, title = {{O}n automorphisms of undirected {B}ruhat graphs}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2204.09174}, note = {arXiv:2204.09174v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nThe (directed) Bruhat graph $\\hat{\\Gamma}(u,v)$ has the elements of the Bruhat interval $[u,v]$ as vertices, with directed edges given by multiplication by a reflection. Famously, $\\hat{\\Gamma}(e,v)$ is regular if and only if the Schubert variety $X_v$ is smooth, and this condition on $v$ is characterized by pattern avoidance. In this work, we classify when the undirected Bruhat graph $\\Gamma(e,v)$ is vertex-transitive; surprisingly this class of permutations is also characterized by pattern avoidance and sits nicely between the classes of smooth permutations and self-dual permutations. This leads us to a general investigation of automorphisms of $\\Gamma(u,v)$ in the course of which we show that special matchings, which originally appeared in the theory Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials, can be characterized as certain $\\Gamma(u,v)$-automorphisms which are conjecturally sufficient to generate the orbit of $e$ under $Aut(\\Gamma(e,v))$.\n\n  * Repeatable patterns and the maximum multiplicity of a generator in a reduced word\n\nChristian Gaetz, Yibo Gao, Pakawut Jiradilok, Gleb Nenashev, Alexander Postnikov\n\nApr 08 2022 math.CO arXiv:2204.03033v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2204.03033, author = {Christian Gaetz and Yibo Gao and Pakawut Jiradilok and Gleb Nenashev and Alexander Postnikov}, title = {{R}epeatable patterns and the maximum multiplicity of a generator in a reduced word}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2204.03033}, note = {arXiv:2204.03033v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe study the maximum multiplicity $\\mathcal{M}(k,n)$ of a simple transposition $s_k=(k \\: k+1)$ in a reduced word for the longest permutation $w_0=n \\: n-1 \\: \\cdots \\: 2 \\: 1$, a problem closely related to much previous work on sorting networks and on the \"$k$-set\" problem. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Pons_J in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3APons_J+in%3Amath)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; @misc{2605.10926, author = {Pau Vives and Anna de Mier and Gabriel Cardona and Joan Carles Pons}, title = {{C}ounting {S}pinal {T}ree-{C}hild {N}etworks via {W}ord {E}ncodings and {G}enerating {F}unctions}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2605.10926}, note = {arXiv:2605.10926v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... @misc{2109.10251, author = {Sungsik Kong and Joan Carles Pons and Laura Kubatko and Kristina Wicke}, title = {{C}lasses of {E}xplicit {P}hylogenetic {N}etworks and their {B}iological and {M}athematical {S}ignificance}, year = {2021}, eprint = {2109.10251}, note = {arXiv:2109.10251v2} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... We show in detail that Dirac's analysis was incomplete and, in completing it, we prove that the fulfilment of Dirac's conjecture --in the \"non-pathological\" cases-- does not imply any need to modify the dynamics.\nWe introduce a bivariate polynomial encoding that incorporates branch lengths directly into a recursive computation from the leaf vertices to the root vertex of a tree. We prove that, for rooted trees with branch lengths and no vertices of degree two, which include all standard phylogenetic trees, two trees have the same polynomial if and only if their underlying unlabeled trees are isomorphic and the branch lengths of corresponding edges are equal. We apply the polynomial encoding to three published HIV-1 phylogenies sampled in different epidemiological settings and show that it accurately separates the three datasets based on their tree topologies and branch lengths, outperforming previous polynomial-based approaches for analyzing rooted trees with branch lengths.\n\n  * Counting Spinal Tree-Child Networks via Word Encodings and Generating Functions\n\nPau Vives, Anna de Mier, Gabriel Cardona, Joan Carles Pons\n\nMay 12 2026 math.CO arXiv:2605.10926v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2605.10926, author = {Pau Vives and Anna de Mier and Gabriel Cardona and Joan Carles Pons}, title = {{C}ounting {S}pinal {T}ree-{C}hild {N}etworks via {W}ord {E}ncodings and {G}enerating {F}unctions}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2605.10926}, note = {arXiv:2605.10926v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe study the enumeration of spinal tree-child phylogenetic networks, a rigid family of tree-child networks in which all internal vertices lie on a single root--to--leaf path. We provide two complementary combinatorial frameworks. First, we introduce a word model: unlabeled spinal networks correspond to a suitable class of restricted words with fixed multiplicities, taken modulo a simple relabeling equivalence, which yields an explicit closed enumeration. Second, we develop a symbolic-method approach based on a marked version of trees that admits a clean recursive specification; its boxed-product translation leads to a solvable bivariate generating function and a direct derivation of the coefficients.\n\n  * A $\\mu$-distance for semidirected orchard phylogenetic networks\n\nGerard Ribas, Joan Carles Pons, Cécile Ané\n\nMay 08 2026 math.CO q-bio.PE arXiv:2605.06243v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2605.06243, author = {Gerard Ribas and Joan Carles Pons and Cécile Ané}, title = {{A} $\\mu$-distance for semidirected orchard phylogenetic networks}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2605.06243}, note = {arXiv:2605.06243v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nIn evolutionary biology, phylogenetic networks are now widely used to represent the historical relationships between species and population, when this history includes reticulation events such as hybridization, gene flow and admixture between populations. Semidirected phylogenetic networks are appropriate models when the direction of some edges and the root position are not identifiable from data. Comparing semidirected networks is important in many applications. For rooted and directed networks, a $\\mu$-representation was originally introduced to distinguish tree-child networks, and has since been extended in two different directions: to the larger class of orchard directed networks by adding an extra component that counts paths to reticulations; and to semidirected networks, through an edge-based variant. \nJosep M. Pons\n\nSep 17 2004 physics.hist-ph gr-qc hep-th math-ph math.MP arXiv:physics/0409076v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{physics/0409076, author = {Josep M.~Pons}, title = {{O}n {D}irac's incomplete analysis of gauge transformations}, year = {2004}, eprint = {physics/0409076}, howpublished = {Stud.Hist.Philos.Mod.Phys. 36 (2005) 491-518}, note = {arXiv:physics/0409076v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nDirac's approach to gauge symmetries is discussed. We follow closely the steps that led him from his conjecture concerning the generators of gauge transformations \\it at a given time --to be contrasted with the common view of gauge transformations as maps from solutions of the equations of motion into other solutions-- to his decision to artificially modify the dynamics, substituting the extended Hamiltonian (including all first-class constraints) for the total Hamiltonian (including only the primary first-class constraints). We show in detail that Dirac's analysis was incomplete and, in completing it, we prove that the fulfilment of Dirac's conjecture --in the \"non-pathological\" cases-- does not imply any need to modify the dynamics. We give a couple of simple but significant examples.\n\n  * Generally covariant theories: the Noether obstruction for realizing certain space-time diffeomorphisms in phase space\n\nJosep M Pons\n\nJun 10 2003 gr-qc hep-th math-ph math.MP arXiv:gr-qc/0306035v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{gr-qc/0306035, author = {Josep M Pons}, title = {{G}enerally covariant theories: the {N}oether obstruction for realizing certain space-time diffeomorphisms in phase space}, year = {2003}, eprint = {gr-qc/0306035}, howpublished = {Class.Quant.Grav. 20 (2003) 3279-3294}, doi = {10.1088/0264-9381/20/15/301}, note = {arXiv:gr-qc/0306035v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe $Z$-Curve as an $n$-Dimensional Hypersphere: Properties and Analysis | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/384699780_The_Z-Curve_as_an_n-Dimensional_Hypersphere_Properties_and_Analysis)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2410.04611 ... In this research, we introduce what seems to be a new mathematical object resulting from projecting the n-dimensional Z-curve onto an n-dimensional sphere.\n\n# The Z-Curve as an n-Dimensional Hypersphere: Properties and Analysis\n\n  * October 2024\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2410.04611\n\nAuthors:\n\nDiego Vazquez Gonzalez\n\nDiego Vazquez Gonzalez\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nHsing-Kuo Pao\n\n  * National Taiwan University of Science and Technology\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this research, we introduce what seems to be a new mathematical object resulting from projecting the n-dimensional Z-curve onto an n-dimensional sphere. The first part presents the algorithm that enables this transition, and the second part focuses on studying the properties of the resulting object.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Cardona_G in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3ACardona_G+in%3Amath)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; @misc{2605.10926, author = {Pau Vives and A